<article class="env-remark" id="03UE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03UE">Remark <span data-tag="03UE">64.15.10</span>.</a> Let us try to illustrate the content of the formula of Lemma <a href="/tag/03UC" data-tag="03UC">64.15.8</a>. Suppose that $\Lambda $, viewed as a trivial $\Gamma $-module, admits a finite resolution $ 0\to P_ r\to \ldots \to P_1 \to P_0\to \Lambda \to 0 $ by some $\Lambda [\Gamma ]$-modules $P_ i$ which are finite and projective as $\Lambda [G]$-modules. In that case </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  H_*\left(\left(P_\bullet \right)_ G\right) = \text{Tor}_*^{\Lambda [G]}\left(\Lambda , \Lambda \right) = H_*(G, \Lambda )  \]
</div>
  <p> and </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{Tr}_\Lambda ^{Z_\gamma }\left(\gamma , P_\bullet \right) =\frac{1}{\#  Z_\gamma }\text{Tr}_\Lambda (\gamma , P_\bullet )=\frac{1}{\#  Z_\gamma }\text{Tr}(\gamma , \Lambda ) = \frac{1}{\#  Z_\gamma }.  \]
</div>
  <p> Therefore, Lemma <a href="/tag/03UC" data-tag="03UC">64.15.8</a> says </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{Tr}_\Lambda (1 , P_ G) = \text{Tr}\left(1 |_{H_*(G, \Lambda )}\right) = {\sum _{\gamma \mapsto 1}}'\frac{1}{\#  Z_\gamma }.  \]
</div>
  <p> This can be interpreted as a point count on the stack $BG$. If $\Lambda = \mathbf{F}_\ell $ with $\ell $ prime to $\#  G$, then $H_*(G, \Lambda )$ is $\mathbf{F}_\ell $ in degree 0 (and $0$ in other degrees) and the formula reads </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  1 = \sum \nolimits _{ \frac{\sigma \text{-conjugacy}}{\text{classes}\langle \gamma \rangle } } \frac{1}{\#  Z_\gamma } \mod \ell .  \]
</div>
  <p> This is in some sense a “trivial” trace formula for $G$. Later we will see that (<a href="/tag/03U0" data-tag="03U0">64.14.3.1</a>) can in some cases be viewed as a highly nontrivial trace formula for a certain type of group, see Section <a href="/tag/03VO" data-tag="03VO">64.30</a>. </p>
</article>